From cddce9adc24267190bb8b1402e269444532304bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 13:24:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: batch sections/facts into one Lara call per experience
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Root cause of "it's translating but takes too long": syncFactTranslations
and syncSectionTranslations each make one Lara API call per row. An
experience with 7 facts and 2 sections meant ~9 separate network
round-trips just for that one experience, on top of the content call —
network latency, not translation compute, was most of the wall-clock
time.

syncFullExperienceTranslations now calls two new batch helpers
(syncSectionsBatch, syncFactsBatch) that translate all of an
experience's sections (or facts) in a single Lara call per locale,
built as one combined field map (title_0/description_0/title_1/...),
then split the result back per row for the upsert. Rows whose hash
already matches an "ok" translation are excluded from the batch, same
skip logic as before, just checked in one bulk query instead of one
per row.

This drops each experience from up to ~10 Lara calls to 3 (content,
sections, facts), so the route's cross-experience concurrency in
/api/translate-proposal-experiences goes from 2 to 4 — cumulative load
stays well under the level that caused rate-limit failures, while
finishing several times faster.

The single-row syncSectionTranslations/syncFactTranslations functions
are unchanged and still used as-is by the admin single-item route
(/api/translate-experience), where translating one row at a time is
already correct.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XBj5YyCv7itzLP8BDyrEj5
